    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Think of any complex sound wave, like music, as being built from simple sine waves of different pitches. The Fourier Transform essentially acts like a sophisticated musical spectrograph, breaking down that complicated wave into all the individual, pure sine wave components it's made of. It tells you the exact \"recipe\"—which frequencies (pitches) are present and how loud each one is. Thus, the original function implicitly contains its constituent harmonic ingredients, and the transform extracts them for you.",
