    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense with the music analogy, but i'm still stuck on the \"hidden\" part. if i'm looking at a graph of a signal that just looks like a messy, random squiggle, how is there actually a pattern of specific frequencies hiding in there? how can we be sure we're finding the real parts and not just seeing things that aren't there?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Your \"messy squiggle\" is just a compact way of representing the *sum* of many simple, hidden oscillations happening at different rates. Mathematically, the transform isn't *assuming* frequencies; it's calculating how effectively a perfect sinusoid of a particular frequency fits into the messy signal. If the signal truly contains a $50\\text{ Hz}$ component, the calculation will produce a high peak at $50\\text{ Hz}$ in the frequency domain. This process is rigorous enough that prominent peaks generally correspond to true structural components of the original signal.",
      "source": "target",
