      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, random noise, like white noise, spreads its energy relatively evenly across all possible frequencies, showing up as a broad, low-level \"hiss\" across the frequency spectrum. Signal processing addresses this by leveraging the concept of signal-to-noise ratio. You look for distinct, tall peaks—which represent structured, strong frequencies—and distinguish them from the general, unpredictable background smear of the noise. Strong, concentrated peaks are usually signals, while flat distributions are often just noise.",
